 "Freshly picked apples from the local orchard are usually sorted into categories based on their size, shape, color, and quality. The farmer will typically start by separating the apples into different piles, with the largest apples going at the top and the smallest at the bottom. The apples are then further sorted based on their color and quality, with the reddest and most perfect apples going into the top piles and the greenest and most imperfect ones going into the bottom piles. The farmer might also consider the apples' stem ends, with the apples having the straightest and cleanest stem ends going into the top piles and the ones with the crookedest and dirtiest stem ends going into the bottom piles. Finally, the farmer will package the apples according to their intended use, such as for eating fresh, making cider, or baking pies. Sorting apples can be a labor-intensive process, but it is an important step in ensuring the apples are sold at their best quality and are safe to eat. The farmer's sorting method can also be influenced by market demand and the intended use of the apples, which may dictate the specific quality and size of apples that are sorted into each category." (Source: U.S. Apple Association)
